<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Learning Target 6 Practice</title>

    <p>
        <em>Create and translate sets using set builder notation and fluently work with sets written in set builder notation.</em>
    </p>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Need to know</title>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Given a set in set-builder notation, write in roster notation (if possible).
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Given a set in roster notation, write in set-builder notation.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs>
        <title>Practice</title>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Given any integer <m>x</m>, does <m>x\mid 0</m>?
                    does <m>0\mid x</m>?
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Yes, for <m>x\in\mathbb{Z}</m>, <m>x\mid 0</m> since
                    <m>0</m> is an integer and <m>a\cdot 0 = 0</m>.
                    However, in the definition of <m>a\mid b</m>, we prohibit
                    <m>a</m> from being <m>0</m>.
                    Moreover, there is no integer <m>k</m> such that
                    <m>0k = x</m>, unless <m>x = 0</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Give examples of integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that
                    <m>a\equiv b \pmod{15}</m> and
                    <m>a\not\equiv b\pmod{15}</m>.
                    Explain using the definition.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Note <m>47 \equiv 17 \pmod{5}</m> since
                    <m>15\cdot 2 = 30= 48-17</m> and <m>2\in \mathbb{Z}</m>, so
                    <m>5 \mid (48-17)</m>.
                    However, <m>47\not\equiv 16\pmod{15}</m> since
                    <m>15\nmid 29 = 47-16.</m> (Lots of correct answers here.)
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Give examples of integers <m>a</m> and <m>b</m> such that
                    <m>a\mid b</m> and <m>a\nmid b</m>.
                    Explain using the definition.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
              <p>
                There are lots of answers here. E.g., you could state that <m>7\mid 21</m> but <m>7\nmid 20</m>. 
                We know <m>7\mid 21</m> as <m>21 = 7\cdot 3</m> and <m>3\in\mathbb{Z}</m>. However, <m>7\nmid 20</m>
                since there is no integer <m>k</m> such that <m>20 = 7\cdot k</m>.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    List all integers <m>a</m> such that <m>a\mid 24</m>.
                    Explain using the definition.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
              <p>
                The integers are <m>-24, -12, -8, -6, -4, -3, -2, -1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 8, 12, 24</m>.
              </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Give an example of two integers, <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, such
                    that <m>a\equiv b \pmod{5}</m>, where one of the integers is
                    negative.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    Let <m>a = 7</m> and <m>b = -13</m>.
                    Then <m>a\equiv b \pmod{5}</m>, or
                    <m>7 \equiv -13 \pmod{5}</m> since
                    <m>5\cdot 4 = 20 = 7 - (-13)</m> and <m>4\in\mathbb{Z}</m>
                    so <m>5 \mid 7-(-13)</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </exercise>
    </paragraphs>
</handout>